    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §270.15, CP L (as proposed in S.6239 & A.7495)",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2674\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to protective\norders for juries\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2024,\nc.622.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter by requiring a court\nfinding that there is good cause to believe that a threat to the safety\nor integrity of the jury exists to, either upon motion by either party\nor on the court's own initiative, issue a juror protective order.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nClick here\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to approval memo 60 of 2024, the Executive stated that the good\ncause standard needed appropriate defining. This legislation is a nego-\ntiated change to the underlying chapter that addresses those concerns.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 622 of the Laws of 2024. S.6239 and A.7495\nboth passed the Senate and Assembly, respectively, in May 2024.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nClick here\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nClick here\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
